WEBVTT

NOTE Visual text from a silent video.

1
00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:07.400
The equipment converts much of the electricity it uses into heat.

2
00:00:07.400 --> 00:00:14.100
If it generates heat faster than it rejects it, energy accumulates and temperature rises.

3
00:00:14.100 --> 00:00:24.000
At equilibrium, thermal inputs and outputs must balance.

4
00:00:24.700 --> 00:00:31.400
Assume net heat rejection of 500 watts per square metre.

5
00:00:31.400 --> 00:00:38.100
Rejecting 2 megawatts then requires 4,000 square metres under that assumption.

6
00:00:38.100 --> 00:00:48.000
Real capacity depends on temperature, surface properties and absorbed heat.

7
00:00:48.700 --> 00:00:55.400
Two panels with the same instantaneous power need not deliver the same energy.

8
00:00:55.400 --> 00:01:02.100
Illumination and eclipses change how long they generate power.

9
00:01:02.100 --> 00:01:10.000
The design must also cover periods without generation.

10
00:01:10.700 --> 00:01:17.400
A ground station sees only part of a low Earth orbit.

11
00:01:17.400 --> 00:01:24.100
Data waits outside contact and can be downlinked during a pass.

12
00:01:24.100 --> 00:01:32.000
More stations or relays change coverage but add infrastructure.

13
00:01:32.700 --> 00:01:39.400
Here, 1 Gbit/s for 300 seconds allows at most 37.5 GB.

14
00:01:39.400 --> 00:01:46.100
A 60 GB dataset does not fit; a 20 GB result fits that ideal budget.

15
00:01:46.100 --> 00:01:56.000
Protocols, errors and other users reduce useful capacity.

16
00:01:56.700 --> 00:02:03.400
Radiation can change a memory state or damage components.

17
00:02:03.400 --> 00:02:10.100
Error correction and redundancy help within their limits.

18
00:02:10.100 --> 00:02:18.000
An unrecoverable failure needs another plan; maintenance is not free.

